  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warming temperatures into the weekend with mainly hit and miss\n showers or thunderstorms.\n\nSeveral shortwave-troughs will traverse the region over the\nweekend, while at the surface, light southeasterly flow around\nretreating New England surface high will allow for moisture\nreturn into the area. Isolated showers are possible tonight\nacross southwest areas as low-level moisture returns into\nthe area. Warming temperatures and increasing instability may\nresult in isold to sct showers and thunderstorms Sat into Sun,\nmainly west of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Moisture and\ninstability increase further on Monday with greater coverage and\nconvection spreading east of the Blue Ridge Mountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Returning to a summer-like pattern next week.\n\nEnsemble model guidance continues to favor a highly amplified \npattern across the country next week. Mid/upper heights should \ncontinue to build across the Desert Southwest into the Great Plains \nregion. At the same time, below average heights persist near Hudson \nBay while extending down along the Eastern Seaboard as an elongated \ntrough. This would support a mainly northwesterly flow regime as \nimpulses track up and over the sprawling upper ridge across the \nmiddle of the U.S. These often have a tendency to produce convective \ncomplexes, some of which result in mesoscale convective systems \n(MCSs). Thus, expect some uptick in convective chances during \nearly portions of next week.\n\nOne more formidable shortwave emerges on Tuesday which will be \ncharacterized by falling heights/pressures as well as increasing \nmoisture advection. This coincides with ample instability rising \ninto the 1,500 to 2,500 J/kg range. With enough vertical shear and \nheight falls, global models support an active day of thunderstorm \nactivity. Ensembles show some sagging of the surface isobaric \npattern with its position indicative of a lee-side trough. This will \nlikely be one focus for convection along with terrain circulations \nand the upstream cold front. Damaging wind gusts and torrential \ndownpours are possible with any such storm. Given the later arrival \nof the front, the threat for storms may persist beyond the core \ndiurnal heating hours.\n\nModels show this frontal zone sagging southward to near the Virginia-\nNorth Carolina border by mid-week. A shift to post-frontal \nnorthwesterlies should gradually quell the threat for thunderstorms. \nDew points begin to fall with temperatures remaining seasonable. \nHigh temperatures largely top out in the upper 80s to low 90s which \nis close to climatology for late July. Overnight lows fall into the \n60s most nights.",
